> According to https://www.sqlite.org/datatype3.html SQLite doesn't support timestamp data type. We must only have types supported by underlying database to prevent confusion / possible future types added by the database driver itself.

Various databases have various types.
They are not always using same name for similar or same type.
That's why we need to normalize column type.
And typeorm already provide a way to normalize columns in AbstractSqliteDriver.ts


```
    normalizeType(column: {
        type?: ColumnType
        length?: number | string
        precision?: number | null
        scale?: number
    }): string {
        if (column.type === Number || column.type === "int") {
            return "integer"
        } else if (column.type === String) {
            return "varchar"
        } else if (column.type === Date) {
            return "datetime"
        } else if (column.type === Boolean) {
            return "boolean"
        } else if (column.type === "uuid") {
            return "varchar"
        } else if (column.type === "simple-array") {
            return "text"
        } else if (column.type === "simple-json") {
            return "text"
        } else if (column.type === "simple-enum") {
            return "varchar"
        } else {
            return (column.type as string) || ""
        }
    }
```

> Can we at least have a way to set this up once instead of having to use a transformer for each column in each entity? This would help for situations where you want to run integration tests with SQLite when your production app uses a standard RDBMS like Postgres etc..

```ts
@Column({ 
   type: DATABASE_TYPE === "sqlite" ? "bigint" : "timestamp"
})
date: number | string
```

define DATABASE_TYPE somewhere in your code, or even in env variable for your particular application. There can be other ways of doing that, it's just one way here.

